should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\n# Example Input\n-----------\nText:\n\nEntities\n\nid,entity,description\n5,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n6,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is an organization that is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n\nRelationships\n\nid,source,target,description\n37,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,Verdant Oasis Plaza is the location of the Unity March\n38,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,Harmony Assembly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }\n    ]\n}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n51,FISHER-COCHRAN THEOREM,\"The Fisher-Cochran Theorem is a fundamental statistical principle in the field of Design of Experiments (DOE) and Analysis of Variance (ANOVA). It serves to elucidate the relationship between the total sum of squares, the sum of squares for treatments, and the sum of squares for error, thereby providing a framework for understanding variance in experimental data.\n\n### Definition and Purpose\nThe Fisher-Cochran theorem establishes a theoretical foundation for partitioning the total variability observed in a dataset into components attributable to different sources, specifically treatments and random error. This partitioning is crucial for conducting ANOVA, as it allows researchers to assess the significance of treatment effects while controlling for variability.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\nMathematically, the theorem can be expressed in the context of ANOVA as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Total Sum of Squares (SST)} = \\text{Sum of Squares for Treatments (SSTr)} + \\text{Sum of Squares for Error (SSE)}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- SST represents the total variability in the data.\n- SSTr quantifies the variability due to the treatment effects.\n- SSE captures the variability due to random error.\n\n### Design Layout\nThe Fisher-Cochran theorem is typically applied within the framework of a balanced ANOVA design, where the experimental units are randomly assigned to different treatment groups. The design layout can vary, but it often involves a factorial or completely randomized design structure.\n\n### Assumptions Required for Validity\nFor the Fisher-Cochran theorem to hold, several assumptions must be satisfied:\n1. The observations are independent.\n2. The data within each treatment group are normally distributed.\n3. The variances among the treatment groups are equal (homogeneity of variance).\n\n### Applicability and Selection Criteria\nThe Fisher-Cochran theorem is applicable in situations where researchers seek to compare means across multiple groups or treatments. It is particularly useful when the experimental design is balanced, and the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance are met. Researchers should select this theorem when conducting ANOVA to ensure valid inference about treatment effects.\n\n### Analysis Procedure\nThe analysis procedure involves calculating the total sum of squares, the sum of squares for treatments, and the sum of squares for error. These values are then used to compute the F-statistic, which is the ratio of the mean square for treatments to the mean square for error. The F-statistic is compared against a critical value from the F-distribution to determine the significance of treatment effects.\n\n### Advantages and Strengths\nThe Fisher-Cochran theorem provides a clear and systematic approach to understanding variance in experimental data. Its strengths include:\n- Facilitating the comparison of multiple treatment means.\n- Allowing for the identification of significant treatment effects while controlling for error.\n- Providing a basis for further statistical inference and hypothesis testing.\n\n### Limitations and Disadvantages\nDespite its strengths, the Fisher-Cochran theorem has limitations:\n- It relies heavily on the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variance, which, if violated, can lead to incorrect conclusions.\n- It may not be suitable for unbalanced designs or when the number of observations per treatment group varies significantly.\n\n### Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives\nThe Fisher-Cochran theorem is closely related to other statistical methods, including:\n- Tukey's HSD (Honestly Significant Difference) test for post-hoc analysis.\n- The Bonferroni correction for multiple comparisons.\n- Non-parametric alternatives such as the Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions are not met.\n\n### Practical Implementation Considerations\nWhen implementing the Fisher-Cochran theorem in practice, researchers should ensure that their experimental design adheres to the necessary assumptions. It is also important to conduct diagnostic checks for normality and homogeneity of variance prior to analysis.\n\n### Software Implementation\nThe Fisher-Cochran theorem is commonly implemented in statistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python's SciPy library, which provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and calculating the necessary sums of squares and F-statistics.\n\n### Common Applications or Examples\nThe Fisher-Cochran theorem is widely used in various fields, including agriculture, medicine, and social sciences, where researchers often compare the effects of different treatments or interventions on a response variable. For example, it may be applied in clinical trials to assess the efficacy of different drug treatments or in agricultural studies to evaluate the impact of various fertilizers on crop yield. \n\nIn summary, the Fisher-Cochran theorem is a pivotal concept in the analysis of variance, providing a structured approach to understanding and analyzing variability in experimental data.\",2\r\n117,TSS,,6\r\n112,SSE,\"**Sum of Squares Error (SSE)**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**:  \nSum of Squares Error (SSE) is a statistical measure that quantifies the amount of variation in a dataset that is not explained by the estimation model. It serves as a critical component in the analysis of variance (ANOVA), providing insight into the discrepancies between observed data and the predictions made by the model. SSE is essential for assessing the goodness of fit of a model and for determining the significance of treatment effects in experimental designs.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**:  \nSSE is mathematically defined as the sum of the squared differences between the observed values and the predicted values (or group means) of the model. The formula can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\nSSE = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(y_i\\) represents the observed values, \\(\\hat{y}_i\\) represents the predicted values from the model, and \\(n\\) is the total number of observations.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**:  \nIn the context of experimental designs, SSE is calculated as part of the ANOVA framework, which may include various designs such as completely randomized designs, randomized block designs, or Latin square designs. Each of these designs has its own structure for grouping and analyzing data, but they all incorporate SSE as a measure of unexplained variation.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**:  \nThe validity of SSE relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The data points must be independent of one another.\n2. Normality: The residuals (differences between observed and predicted values) should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among the groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**:  \nSSE is applicable in any scenario where a model is fitted to data, particularly in the context of ANOVA. It is used to evaluate the effectiveness of different treatments or factors in an experiment. Researchers should consider using SSE when they need to assess the unexplained variation in their data and when comparing models or treatments.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**:  \nTo analyze SSE within the framework of ANOVA, the following steps are typically followed:\n1. Fit the model to the data and calculate the predicted values.\n2. Compute the SSE using the formula provided.\n3. Use SSE to calculate the Mean Square Error (MSE) by dividing SSE by the degrees of freedom associated with the error.\n4. Compare MSE to the Mean Square Treatment (MST) to determine the significance of treatment effects using an F-test.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**:  \n- SSE provides a clear measure of the unexplained variation in the data, which is crucial for model evaluation.\n- It is a fundamental component of ANOVA, allowing for the comparison of multiple groups.\n- SSE helps in identifying the adequacy of the model and the presence of significant treatment effects.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**:  \n- SSE alone does not provide information about the direction or magnitude of effects; it only quantifies unexplained variation.\n- It is sensitive to outliers, which can disproportionately affect the SSE value.\n- SSE does not account for the structure of the data, such as potential interactions between factors.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**:  \nSSE is closely related to other statistical measures such as Total Sum of Squares (SST) and Sum of Squares for Treatments (SSTr). Alternatives to SSE in model evaluation include R-squared and adjusted R-squared, which provide additional context regarding the proportion of variance explained by the model.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**:  \nWhen implementing SSE in practice, researchers should ensure that the assumptions of the ANOVA are met. It is also important to conduct residual analysis to check for normality and homogeneity of variance. Proper experimental design and randomization techniques should be employed to minimize bias.\n\n**Software Implementation**:  \nSSE can be easily calculated using statistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python. Most of these software tools provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA, which automatically compute SSE as part of the output.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**:  \nSSE is commonly used in various fields, including agriculture (to evaluate the effects of different fertilizers), medicine (to assess treatment efficacy), and industrial experiments (to optimize processes). It is particularly useful in comparative studies where multiple treatments or conditions are analyzed simultaneously.\",7\r\n113,F-STATISTIC,\"### F-Statistic: Comprehensive Summary\n\n**Definition and Purpose**  \nThe F-statistic is a critical component in the analysis of variance (ANOVA), serving as a ratio that compares the variance between group means to the variance within groups. Its primary purpose is to determine whether there are statistically significant differences among the means of different groups in an experiment.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**  \nThe F-statistic is mathematically defined as:\n\n\\[\nF = \\frac{\\text{Variance Between Groups}}{\\text{Variance Within Groups}} = \\frac{MS_{between}}{MS_{within}}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(MS_{between}\\) is the mean square between groups, calculated as the sum of squares between groups divided by its degrees of freedom, and \\(MS_{within}\\) is the mean square within groups, calculated as the sum of squares within groups divided by its degrees of freedom.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure**  \nThe F-statistic is typically employed in the context of a completely randomized design, randomized block design, or factorial design, where multiple groups or treatments are compared. The layout involves grouping observations into distinct categories based on the treatment applied, allowing for the assessment of variance across these groups.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**  \nFor the F-statistic to be valid, several assumptions must be met:\n1. Independence of observations: Each observation must be independent of others.\n2. Normality: The data within each group should be approximately normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the groups should be equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nThe F-statistic is applicable in situations where researchers aim to compare the means of three or more groups. It is particularly useful when the experimental design involves multiple treatments or factors, and the researcher seeks to understand the impact of these factors on a response variable.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis procedure involving the F-statistic typically includes:\n1. Formulating the null hypothesis (H0: all group means are equal) and the alternative hypothesis (H1: at least one group mean is different).\n2. Calculating the F-statistic using the mean squares.\n3. Comparing the calculated F-statistic to a critical value from the F-distribution table, based on the degrees of freedom and the chosen significance level (α).\n4. Making a decision to reject or fail to reject the null hypothesis.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nThe F-statistic provides a robust method for testing differences among group means, especially when dealing with multiple groups. It allows for the simultaneous comparison of several means, reducing the risk of Type I error that would arise from conducting multiple t-tests.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nThe F-statistic is sensitive to violations of its underlying assumptions, particularly the assumption of normality and homogeneity of variances. If these assumptions are not met, the results may be misleading. Additionally, the F-statistic does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated methods include t-tests for comparing two groups, as well as non-parametric alternatives such as the Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions of ANOVA are violated. Factorial ANOVA and repeated measures ANOVA are also related designs that utilize the F-statistic for more complex experimental setups.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen implementing the F-statistic in practice, researchers should ensure that the assumptions are checked and met. Data transformation techniques may be applied if normality or homogeneity of variances is violated. Additionally, the choice of significance level (α) should be carefully considered based on the context of the study.\n\n**Software Implementation**  \nThe F-statistic can be easily computed using statistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python's SciPy library, which provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and calculating the F-statistic.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**  \nCommon applications of the F-statistic include agricultural experiments comparing crop yields across different fertilizers, clinical trials assessing the effectiveness of various treatments, and industrial experiments evaluating the performance of different manufacturing processes.\",5\r\n110,SSA,\"**Entity: Sum of Squares for Factor A (SSA)**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nThe Sum of Squares for Factor A (SSA) is a statistical measure used in the context of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to quantify the variation in the response variable that can be attributed to the first factor (or treatment) in an experimental design. It is a crucial component of the ANOVA table, which summarizes the sources of variation in the data and helps in determining whether the factor has a statistically significant effect on the response variable.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nMathematically, SSA is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\nSSA = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} n_i (\\bar{Y}_{i} - \\bar{Y})^2\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(k\\) is the number of levels of Factor A,\n- \\(n_i\\) is the number of observations at level \\(i\\),\n- \\(\\bar{Y}_{i}\\) is the mean response at level \\(i\\),\n- \\(\\bar{Y}\\) is the overall mean response across all levels.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nSSA is typically assessed within the framework of a completely randomized design or a randomized block design, where the experimental units are assigned to different treatment levels of Factor A. The design structure involves multiple levels of Factor A, with observations collected for each level.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of SSA relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence: The observations must be independent of one another.\n2. Normality: The residuals (differences between observed and predicted values) should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of Variance: The variances among the groups (levels of Factor A) should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nSSA is applicable in situations where researchers are interested in understanding the effect of one or more categorical factors on a continuous response variable. It is particularly useful when comparing means across different treatment groups to identify significant differences.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure involves:\n1. Calculating the total sum of squares (TSS) for the response variable.\n2. Partitioning TSS into components, including SSA, to assess the contribution of Factor A.\n3. Conducting an F-test to determine if the variation explained by Factor A is statistically significant compared to the residual variation.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- SSA provides a clear quantification of the variation explained by Factor A, facilitating the interpretation of treatment effects.\n- It is a foundational component of ANOVA, allowing for the comparison of multiple groups simultaneously.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- SSA is sensitive to violations of the underlying assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variance, which can lead to misleading results.\n- It does not provide information about the nature of the differences between groups; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nSSA is part of the broader ANOVA framework, which includes other components such as Sum of Squares for Error (SSE) and Total Sum of Squares (TSS). Alternatives to ANOVA, such as non-parametric tests (e.g., Kruskal-Wallis test), may be considered when assumptions are violated.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing SSA in practice, researchers should ensure that the experimental design is appropriately structured to meet the assumptions of ANOVA. Adequate sample sizes and randomization techniques should be employed to enhance the reliability of the results.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nSSA can be computed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries such as Statsmodels. These tools provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and generating the ANOVA table, which includes SSA.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nCommon applications of SSA include agricultural experiments comparing crop yields across different fertilizer treatments, clinical trials assessing the effectiveness of various medications, and industrial experiments evaluating the impact of different manufacturing processes on product quality.\",3\r\n111,SSB,\"**Sum of Squares for Blocks (SSB)**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**: The Sum of Squares for Blocks (SSB) is a statistical measure used in Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to quantify the variation attributable to blocking factors in an experimental design. Blocking is a technique employed to reduce the impact of nuisance variables by grouping experimental units that are similar, thereby allowing for a more accurate assessment of the treatment effects.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**: In the context of a two-way ANOVA without interaction, SSB can be calculated using the formula:\n\\[\nSSB = \\sum_{j=1}^{b} n_j (\\bar{Y}_{j.} - \\bar{Y}_{..})^2\n\\]\nwhere \\( n_j \\) is the number of observations in block \\( j \\), \\( \\bar{Y}_{j.} \\) is the mean of the observations in block \\( j \\), and \\( \\bar{Y}_{..} \\) is the overall mean of all observations. Here, \\( b \\) represents the total number of blocks.\n\n**Design Layout**: SSB is typically utilized in block designs, such as randomized block designs (RBD), where experimental units are grouped into blocks based on a blocking factor. Each block contains all treatment levels, ensuring that the effects of the treatments can be isolated from the variability introduced by the blocks.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**: The validity of SSB relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence: Observations within and between blocks must be independent.\n2. Normality: The residuals should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of Variance: The variances across blocks should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**: SSB is applicable in situations where there are identifiable nuisance factors that can be controlled through blocking. It is particularly useful when the experimental units are heterogeneous, and the researcher aims to reduce variability to enhance the precision of treatment comparisons.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**: In an ANOVA framework, SSB is calculated as part of the overall ANOVA table. The total sum of squares (TSS) is partitioned into components, including SSB, the sum of squares for treatments (SST), and the sum of squares for error (SSE). The F-statistic is then computed to test the significance of the treatment effects while accounting for the variation explained by the blocks.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**: The primary advantage of using SSB in experimental designs is its ability to control for variability due to nuisance factors, leading to more reliable estimates of treatment effects. This can enhance the power of the statistical tests and improve the overall efficiency of the experiment.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**: One limitation of SSB is that it requires a clear understanding of the blocking factors and their levels. If the blocking factors are not appropriately chosen, or if there are too few blocks, the benefits of blocking may be diminished. Additionally, the complexity of the design increases with the number of blocks, which may complicate the analysis.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**: SSB is closely related to other components of ANOVA, such as the Sum of Squares for Treatments (SST) and the Sum of Squares for Error (SSE). Alternatives to blocking include factorial designs and Latin square designs, which may be more suitable in certain experimental contexts.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**: When implementing SSB in an experimental design, researchers should carefully select blocking factors based on prior knowledge of the experimental units. The number of blocks should be sufficient to capture the variability without introducing excessive complexity.\n\n**Software Implementation**: Many statistical software packages, such as R, SAS, and SPSS, provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA, which include the calculation of SSB as part of the ANOVA table output.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**: SSB is commonly used in agricultural experiments, clinical trials, and industrial experiments where the effects of treatments need to be evaluated while controlling for variability due to environmental or other nuisance factors. For instance, in agricultural studies, different plots of land may be treated as blocks to account for soil variability.\",3\r\n1868,DFE,\"DFE stands for Degrees of Freedom for Error, which is used in statistical tests to determine the number of independent values in a calculation.\",1\r\n245,MSE,\"**Mean Squared Error (MSE) Summary**\n\nMean Squared Error (MSE) is a statistical measure that quantifies the average of the squares of the errors or deviations from a predicted value. It serves as a critical component in various statistical analyses, particularly in the context of estimating variance within treatment groups in the Design of Experiments (DOE) and Analysis of Variance (ANOVA).\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nMSE is defined as the average of the squared differences between observed values and the values predicted by a model. It is primarily used to assess the accuracy of a model's predictions and to estimate the variance of the errors in statistical analyses. In the context of ANOVA, MSE is utilized to evaluate the variability within treatment groups, providing insight into the consistency of the data.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nMSE is mathematically formulated as:\n\\[\nMSE = \\frac{SSE}{df}\n\\]\nwhere \\(SSE\\) represents the sum of squares for error, and \\(df\\) denotes the degrees of freedom associated with the error term. In ANOVA, MSE is calculated as the mean square for error, which is derived from the total variability in the data.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn experimental designs such as the Latin square design, MSE is calculated to assess the error variance within the treatment groups. The structure of the design involves arranging treatments in a square format to control for two blocking factors, allowing for a more precise estimation of treatment effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of MSE relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The errors should be independent of one another.\n2. Normality: The errors should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variance: The variance among the groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nMSE is applicable in various statistical contexts, particularly when evaluating the performance of predictive models or when conducting ANOVA to compare means across multiple groups. It is selected when the goal is to quantify the error variance and assess the reliability of the model's predictions.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nIn the context of ANOVA, the analysis procedure involves calculating the total sum of squares, partitioning it into treatment and error components, and then computing MSE as part of the overall analysis. This allows researchers to determine whether the treatment effects are statistically significant.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nMSE provides a straightforward and interpretable measure of error variance, facilitating comparisons across different models and treatments. It is particularly useful in identifying models that provide better predictive accuracy.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nOne limitation of MSE is that it is sensitive to outliers, as squaring the errors can disproportionately increase the measure in the presence of extreme values. Additionally, MSE does not provide information about the direction of the errors, only their magnitude.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nMSE is closely related to other error metrics such as Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE). In the context of ANOVA, it is also associated with other designs like completely randomized designs and factorial designs.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing MSE in practice, it is essential to ensure that the assumptions of independence, normality, and homogeneity of variance are met. If these assumptions are violated, alternative methods or transformations may be necessary.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMSE can be easily calculated using statistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries like NumPy and SciPy, which provide built-in functions for ANOVA and error variance calculations.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nMSE is commonly used in various fields, including machine learning for model evaluation, quality control in manufacturing processes, and clinical trials to assess treatment effects. In experimental designs, it is particularly relevant in agricultural studies, psychological experiments, and any research involving comparative analysis of multiple treatments.\",2\r\n506,REJECT NULL HYPOTHESIS,The decision rule to reject the null hypothesis is based on the comparison of the F statistic with a critical value.,1\r\n508,MEAN SQUARES,\"**Comprehensive Summary of Mean Squares in ANOVA**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nMean squares are a fundamental statistical measure used in Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) to estimate the variance associated with different sources of variation within a dataset. They serve as a critical component in determining whether the means of different groups are statistically significantly different from one another.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nMean squares are calculated by dividing the sum of squares (SS) for a particular source of variation by its corresponding degrees of freedom (df). The general formula for mean squares (MS) is given by:\n\n\\[\nMS = \\frac{SS}{df}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(SS\\) is the sum of squares for the source of variation.\n- \\(df\\) is the degrees of freedom associated with that source.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn the context of ANOVA, mean squares are derived from the overall experimental design, which typically includes factors (independent variables), levels (different conditions or treatments), and the response variable (dependent variable). The layout can be structured as a balanced or unbalanced design, depending on the number of observations per treatment group.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of mean squares in ANOVA relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: Each observation must be independent of others.\n2. Normality: The residuals (differences between observed and predicted values) should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the different groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nMean squares are applicable in any ANOVA framework, including one-way ANOVA, two-way ANOVA, and more complex designs. They are selected when comparing the means of multiple groups to assess whether at least one group mean is different from the others.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure involving mean squares typically includes:\n1. Calculation of the total sum of squares (SST), which is partitioned into treatment sum of squares (SSTr) and error sum of squares (SSE).\n2. Calculation of mean squares for treatments (MST) and error (MSE).\n3. Computation of the F-ratio, which is the ratio of the mean square for treatments to the mean square for error:\n\n\\[\nF = \\frac{MST}{MSE}\n\\]\n\n4. Comparison of the F-ratio to a critical value from the F-distribution to determine statistical significance.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\nMean squares provide a clear and quantifiable measure of variance attributable to different sources, facilitating the comparison of group means. They are essential for hypothesis testing in ANOVA, allowing researchers to make informed decisions based on statistical evidence.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\nThe primary limitations of mean squares arise from the assumptions required for their validity. Violations of normality or homogeneity of variances can lead to inaccurate conclusions. Additionally, mean squares alone do not indicate which specific groups differ; post-hoc tests are necessary for that purpose.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nMean squares are closely related to other statistical measures in ANOVA, such as sum of squares and F-statistics. Alternatives to ANOVA, such as non-parametric tests (e.g., Kruskal-Wallis test), may be considered when assumptions are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing mean squares in practice, researchers should ensure that the assumptions of ANOVA are checked and met. Data transformation or the use of robust statistical methods may be necessary if assumptions are violated.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nMean squares can be easily calculated using statistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries (e.g., statsmodels). These tools provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and reporting mean squares along with F-statistics and p-values.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nMean squares are commonly used in various fields, including agriculture (to compare crop yields across different treatments), medicine (to assess the effectiveness of different drugs), and social sciences (to evaluate the impact of educational interventions). They are integral to experimental designs where multiple groups or conditions are compared.\",1\r\n243,SSTR,\"**Comprehensive Summary of SSTR (Sum of Squares for Treatment)**\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nSSTR, or Sum of Squares for Treatment, is a critical component in the analysis of variance (ANOVA) framework. It quantifies the variation among the means of different treatment groups in an experiment. The primary purpose of SSTR is to assess whether the treatment effects significantly influence the response variable, thereby allowing researchers to determine if the differences observed between group means are statistically significant.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe mathematical formulation of SSTR is expressed as follows:\n\n\\[\nSSTR = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} n_i (\\bar{Y}_i - \\bar{Y})^2\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(k\\) is the number of treatment groups,\n- \\(n_i\\) is the number of observations in the \\(i\\)-th group,\n- \\(\\bar{Y}_i\\) is the mean of the \\(i\\)-th group,\n- \\(\\bar{Y}\\) is the overall mean of all observations.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nSSTR is typically utilized within the context of various experimental designs, including completely randomized designs, randomized block designs, and Latin square designs. In a Latin square design, for instance, treatments are arranged in a two-dimensional grid to control for two blocking factors, allowing for a more efficient estimation of treatment effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nFor SSTR to provide valid results, several assumptions must be met:\n1. Independence: Observations must be independent of one another.\n2. Normality: The response variable should be normally distributed within each treatment group.\n3. Homogeneity of Variance: The variances among the treatment groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nSSTR is applicable in situations where researchers are interested in comparing the means of multiple groups to determine if at least one group mean is different from the others. It is particularly useful in experiments with controlled treatments and when the assumptions of ANOVA are satisfied.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis involving SSTR typically follows these steps:\n1. Calculate the overall mean and the means for each treatment group.\n2. Compute SSTR using the formula provided.\n3. Calculate the total sum of squares (SST) and the sum of squares for error (SSE).\n4. Perform an F-test to determine if the treatment effects are statistically significant by comparing the mean square for treatment (MSTR) to the mean square for error (MSE).\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- SSTR provides a clear measure of treatment effects, facilitating the understanding of how different treatments influence the response variable.\n- It allows for the partitioning of total variability into components attributable to treatments and error, enhancing the interpretability of results.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The validity of SSTR is contingent upon the assumptions of ANOVA being met; violations can lead to incorrect conclusions.\n- It does not indicate which specific treatments are different; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nSSTR is closely related to other components of ANOVA, such as SSE (Sum of Squares for Error) and SST (Total Sum of Squares). Alternative methods for analyzing treatment effects include non-parametric tests (e.g., Kruskal-Wallis test) when ANOVA assumptions are violated.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing SSTR in practice, researchers should ensure that the experimental design is robust and that sample sizes are adequate to meet the assumptions of ANOVA. Additionally, careful planning of the randomization process is essential to maintain the independence of observations.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nSSTR can be computed using various statistical software packages, including R, SAS, SPSS, and Python libraries such as SciPy and StatsModels, which provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA and calculating sums of squares.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nSSTR is commonly used in agricultural experiments to compare the effects of different fertilizers on crop yield, in clinical trials to assess the efficacy of different treatments, and in industrial settings to evaluate the performance of various manufacturing processes.\",2\r\n244,MSTR,\"**Mean Square for Treatments (MSTR)**\n\n**Definition and Purpose**:  \nMean Square for Treatments (MSTR) is a statistical measure used in the analysis of variance (ANOVA) to evaluate the effects of different treatments or factors on a response variable. It quantifies the variability attributed to the treatment effects by averaging the sum of squares for treatments (SSTr) over its associated degrees of freedom. MSTR serves as a critical component in determining whether the treatment means are significantly different from each other.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation**:  \nMSTR is calculated using the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{MSTR} = \\frac{\\text{SSTr}}{\\text{df}_{\\text{Tr}}}\n\\]\n\nwhere SSTr is the sum of squares for treatments, and \\(\\text{df}_{\\text{Tr}}\\) is the degrees of freedom associated with the treatments, typically calculated as \\(k - 1\\) (where \\(k\\) is the number of treatment groups).\n\n**Design Layout**:  \nMSTR is applicable in various experimental designs, including completely randomized designs, randomized block designs, and Latin square designs. In a Latin square design, for instance, treatments are arranged in a two-dimensional grid to control for two blocking factors, allowing for a more efficient estimation of treatment effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity**:  \nThe validity of MSTR relies on several key assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations: The responses must be independent of one another.\n2. Normality: The response variable should be normally distributed within each treatment group.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the treatment groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**:  \nMSTR is used in situations where researchers aim to compare the means of different treatment groups to determine if there are statistically significant differences among them. It is particularly useful in controlled experiments where randomization is employed to mitigate bias.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**:  \nIn the context of ANOVA, MSTR is compared against the Mean Square for Error (MSE) to compute the F-statistic:\n\n\\[\nF = \\frac{\\text{MSTR}}{\\text{MSE}}\n\\]\n\nThis F-statistic is then used to test the null hypothesis that all treatment means are equal.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**:  \n- MSTR provides a clear and quantifiable measure of treatment effects.\n- It facilitates the comparison of multiple treatment groups simultaneously.\n- The use of ANOVA, including MSTR, allows for the control of Type I error rates when testing multiple hypotheses.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**:  \n- The assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances can be restrictive; violations may lead to inaccurate conclusions.\n- MSTR does not indicate which specific treatments are different; post-hoc tests are required for that purpose.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**:  \nMSTR is closely related to other ANOVA components, such as Mean Square for Error (MSE) and the overall F-test. Alternatives to ANOVA, such as non-parametric tests (e.g., Kruskal-Wallis test), may be considered when assumptions are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**:  \nWhen implementing MSTR in practice, researchers should ensure that the experimental design is appropriately randomized and that sample sizes are adequate to meet the assumptions of ANOVA. It is also important to conduct diagnostic checks for normality and homogeneity of variances.\n\n**Software Implementation**:  \nMSTR can be computed using statistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python. These tools typically provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA, which automatically calculate MSTR and other relevant statistics.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples**:  \nMSTR is commonly used in agricultural experiments to compare the effects of different fertilizers on crop yield, in clinical trials to assess the efficacy of various treatments, and in industrial settings to evaluate the performance of different manufacturing processes.\",2\r\n\n\n-----Relationships-----\nhuman_readable_id,source,target,description,combined_degree\r\n47,FISHER-COCHRAN THEOREM,ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE,The Fisher-Cochran theorem is a concept that is relevant in the context of Analysis of Variance,264\r\n545,TSS,ANOVA,ANOVA is used to analyze the total sum of squares (TSS) in the context of statistical experiments,169\r\n126,SSE,TSS,\"### Comprehensive Summary of Total Sum of Squares (TSS) and Error Sum of Squares (SSE)\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nTotal Sum of Squares (TSS) is a fundamental concept in the analysis of variance (ANOVA) that quantifies the total variation present in a dataset. It serves as a measure of the total variability of the response variable around its mean. TSS is partitioned into different components, primarily the treatment sum of squares (SSTr) and the error sum of squares (SSE), which helps in understanding the sources of variation in the data.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\nThe mathematical formulation of TSS can be expressed as:\n\\[\nTSS = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (Y_i - \\bar{Y})^2\n\\]\nwhere \\(Y_i\\) represents the individual observations, \\(\\bar{Y}\\) is the overall mean of the observations, and \\(n\\) is the total number of observations. TSS can also be partitioned as:\n\\[\nTSS = SSTr + SSE\n\\]\nwhere \\(SSTr\\) represents the sum of squares due to treatments and \\(SSE\\) represents the sum of squares due to error.\n\n**Design Layout or Structure:**\nIn the context of experimental designs, TSS is derived from the data collected through various treatments or groups. The layout typically involves random assignment of experimental units to different treatment levels, allowing for the assessment of treatment effects while controlling for variability.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nFor the validity of TSS and its partitioning into SSTr and SSE, several assumptions must be met:\n1. Independence of observations: The data points must be independent of each other.\n2. Normality: The residuals (errors) should be normally distributed.\n3. Homogeneity of variances: The variances among the groups should be approximately equal.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nTSS is applicable in any scenario where the variability of a response variable needs to be analyzed, particularly in comparative studies involving multiple groups or treatments. It is essential in determining whether the observed differences among group means are statistically significant.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure involves calculating TSS, SSTr, and SSE, followed by the computation of the F-statistic to test hypotheses regarding the equality of group means. The F-statistic is calculated as:\n\\[\nF = \\frac{MS_{Tr}}{MS_{E}}\n\\]\nwhere \\(MS_{Tr}\\) is the mean square for treatments and \\(MS_{E}\\) is the mean square for error.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- TSS provides a comprehensive measure of total variability, allowing researchers to understand the extent of variation in their data.\n- The partitioning of TSS into SSTr and SSE aids in identifying the sources of variation, facilitating targeted interventions.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- The assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances can be restrictive; violations may lead to inaccurate conclusions.\n- TSS alone does not provide information about the direction or magnitude of effects; further analysis is required to interpret results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nTSS is closely related to other statistical measures such as the Mean Square Error (MSE) and the R-squared statistic in regression analysis. Alternative methods for analyzing variance include non-parametric tests when assumptions are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing TSS calculations, it is crucial to ensure proper data collection methods and randomization to minimize bias. Additionally, software tools such as R, SAS, and SPSS can facilitate the computation of TSS, SSTr, and SSE, along with the associated statistical tests.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nCommon statistical software packages, including R and SAS, provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA, which automatically calculate TSS, SSTr, and SSE as part of the analysis output.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nTSS and its components are widely used in various fields, including agriculture (to compare crop yields under different treatments), medicine (to assess the effectiveness of different drugs), and social sciences (to evaluate the impact of educational interventions).\",13\r\n127,SSE,F-STATISTIC,\"The F-statistic is calculated using SSE, where a smaller SSE leads to a larger F-statistic, indicating a stronger treatment effect.\",12\r\n122,SSA,TSS,\"TSS is the total variation in the data, which is the sum of SSA, SSB, and SSE, indicating their interrelationship in ANOVA.\",9\r\n124,SSB,TSS,\"TSS is the total variation in the data, which is the sum of SSA, SSB, and SSE, indicating their interrelationship in ANOVA.\",9\r\n2483,SSE,DFE,SSE is calculated using the degrees of freedom for error (DFE) to assess the model's performance,8\r\n2484,MSE,RSQUARE,MSE is related to RSquare as it helps in determining the goodness of fit of the regression model,8\r\n548,REJECT NULL HYPOTHESIS,F-STATISTIC,The decision to reject the null hypothesis is based on the value of the F-statistic,6\r\n550,MEAN SQUARES,F-STATISTIC,Mean squares are used in the calculation of the F-statistic in ANOVA,6\r\n223,SSTR,MSTR,\"### Comprehensive Summary of SSTR and MSTR in Analysis of Variance\n\n**Definition and Purpose:**\nSSTR (Sum of Squares for Treatment) and MSTR (Mean Square for Treatment) are key components in the Analysis of Variance (ANOVA), a statistical method used to compare means across multiple groups. SSTR quantifies the variation in the response variable that can be attributed to the different treatment levels, while MSTR represents the average variation due to treatment effects, providing a basis for hypothesis testing regarding the significance of these effects.\n\n**Mathematical Formulation:**\n- **SSTR** is calculated as:\n  \\[\n  SSTR = \\sum_{i=1}^{k} n_i (\\bar{Y}_i - \\bar{Y})^2\n  \\]\n  where \\( k \\) is the number of treatment groups, \\( n_i \\) is the number of observations in group \\( i \\), \\( \\bar{Y}_i \\) is the mean of group \\( i \\), and \\( \\bar{Y} \\) is the overall mean of all observations.\n\n- **MSTR** is derived from SSTR and is calculated as:\n  \\[\n  MSTR = \\frac{SSTR}{k - 1}\n  \\]\n  where \\( k - 1 \\) represents the degrees of freedom associated with the treatment.\n\n**Design Layout:**\nIn the context of ANOVA, the design layout typically involves a randomized block design or completely randomized design, where treatments are applied to experimental units in a manner that minimizes bias and allows for the assessment of treatment effects.\n\n**Assumptions Required for Validity:**\nThe validity of SSTR and MSTR relies on several assumptions:\n1. Independence of observations.\n2. Normality of the response variable within each treatment group.\n3. Homogeneity of variances across treatment groups (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nSSTR and MSTR are applicable in situations where researchers aim to compare means across multiple groups or treatments. They are particularly useful when the experimental design is structured to allow for the isolation of treatment effects from random variation.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis procedure involves calculating SSTR and MSTR, followed by the computation of the F-statistic:\n\\[\nF = \\frac{MSTR}{MSE}\n\\]\nwhere MSE (Mean Square Error) is derived from the residual sum of squares. This F-statistic is then compared against a critical value from the F-distribution to determine the significance of treatment effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- Provides a systematic approach to testing hypotheses about treatment effects.\n- Allows for the comparison of multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error compared to multiple t-tests.\n- Facilitates the understanding of variance components in experimental data.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- Sensitive to violations of assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- Requires a balanced design (equal sample sizes) for optimal performance, although adjustments can be made for unbalanced designs.\n- Interpretation can be complex, especially in the presence of interactions in factorial designs.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nSSTR and MSTR are integral to various ANOVA designs, including one-way ANOVA, two-way ANOVA, and factorial ANOVA. Alternatives to ANOVA include non-parametric tests such as the Kruskal-Wallis test when assumptions are not met.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nWhen implementing SSTR and MSTR in practice, researchers should ensure that the experimental design is robust and that data collection methods adhere to the assumptions of ANOVA. It is also important to conduct diagnostic checks for normality and homogeneity of variances.\n\n**Software Implementation:**\nStatistical software packages such as R, SAS, SPSS, and Python's SciPy library provide built-in functions for conducting ANOVA, which automatically calculate SSTR, MSTR, and the associated F-statistic.\n\n**Common Applications or Examples:**\nSSTR and MSTR are commonly used in agricultural experiments to compare crop yields across different fertilizers, in clinical trials to assess the effectiveness of various treatments, and in industrial settings to evaluate the impact of different manufacturing processes on product quality.\",4\r\n283,MSTR,FISHER-COCHRAN THEOREM,The Fisher-Cochran theorem relates MSTR to the distribution of treatment effects in the analysis of variance.,4\r\n\n\nThe report should include the following sections:\n\n- TITLE: community's name that represents its key entities - title should be short but specific. When possible, include representative named entities in the title.\n- SUMMARY: An executive summary of the community's overall structure, how its entities are related to each other, and significant information associated with its entities.\n- IMPACT SEVERITY RATING: a float score between 0-10 that represents the severity of IMPACT posed by entities within the community.  IMPACT is the scored importance of a community.\n- RATING EXPLANATION: Give a single sentence explanation of the IMPACT severity rating.\n- DETAILED FINDINGS: A list of 5-10 key insights about the community. Each insight should have a short summary followed by multiple paragraphs of explanatory text grounded according to the grounding rules below. Be comprehensive.\n\nReturn output as a well-formed JSON-formatted string with the following format:\n    {\n        \"title\": <report_title>,\n        \"summary\": <executive_summary>,\n        \"rating\": <impact_severity_rating>,\n        \"rating_explanation\": <rating_explanation>,\n        \"findings\": [\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_1_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_1_explanation>\n            },\n            {\n                \"summary\":<insight_2_summary>,\n                \"explanation\": <insight_2_explanation>\n            }\n        ]\n    }\n\n# Grounding Rules\n\nPoints supported by data should list their data references as follows:\n\n\"This is an example sentence supported by multiple data references [Data: <dataset name> (record ids); <dataset name> (record ids)].\"\n\nDo not list more than 5 record ids in a single reference. Instead, list the top 5 most relevant record ids and add \"+more\" to indicate that there are more.\n\nFor example:\n\"Person X is the owner of Company Y and subject to many allegations of wrongdoing [Data: Reports (1), Entities (5, 7); Relationships (23); Claims (7, 2, 34, 64, 46, +more)].\"\n\nwhere 1, 5, 7, 23, 2, 34, 46, and 64 represent the id (not the index) of the relevant data record.\n\nDo not include information where the supporting evidence for it is not provided.\n\nLimit the total report length to 2500 words.\n\nOutput:", "role": "user"}], "parameters": {"model": "gpt-4o-mini", "frequency_penalty": 0.0, "max_tokens": 4000, "n": 1, "presence_penalty": 0.0, "temperature": 0.0, "top_p": 1.0, "response_format": {"type": "json_object"}}}, "key": "chat_create_community_report_309f3f94a424d0e9015c8d94da7cf6e38ba18d28bdf4c6a4a41c53a688bd53b5_v2"}